tell us a little bit about  the trajectory you have had so that so  that we get a sense of of how you moved  from a solidly middle-class background  through being the CEO of palm and and  getting and growing your wealth base how  did that happen and and is there a  connection to you being an entrepreneur  to your views about inherited wealth  well sure there's there's definitely a  connection there i mean i mean my story  is i went i came to Yale I had never  really been exposed to education like  this I went to a very poor public high  school where I did not get a good  education where was that in Benton  Harbor Michigan and I came here totally  unprepared for the academic rigor of  Yale not like you guys today where you  get so much great prep to come here I I  was really it was a struggle for me in  my first couple of years here to figure  out how to study and how to write papers  and all those kind of things but I just  thrived in it I just loved it I loved  this place I loved everything I could  learn from it and then I went out into  the working world for a couple years I  went to a bank in Philadelphia as a  trainee and loved the business world and  decided that's where I wanted to focus I  went back to business school at Harvard  and got my MBA and I wasn't really sure  what I wanted to do I had been in the  bank and I'd seen entrepreneurs in  action and I got kind of excited then  about entrepreneurship it was a mainly  cable TV systems it was you probably  can't relate to this but in the very  very beginning of cable TV when they  were just installing it was a very  entrepreneurial venture small guys doing  it town by town by town and I was  financing them so I got to know them and  really appreciate them and I decided  that I was really interested in  entrepreneurial activities and then  Apple computer came to campus to recruit  the first time they ever came to Harvard  Business School they were interviewing  for technical people I was not a  technical person so they didn't accept  me for an interview but I went and sat  outside the interview room all day and  every time the interviewer came  out and said you know hi introduce  myself to her and say I'm here to see  you and she said well you're not on my  schedule and I'd say I know but I need  to talk to you because I really need to  come work for Apple so by the end of the  day she agreed to sit down and talk to  me and I ended up being the only person  who was hired from the Harvard Business  School to go to apples so it was kind of  interesting and I ended up in a  logistics role in operations where I  won't go through all the details because  it goes on and on but I just I loved the  fast pace i love the innovation i loved  it was a crazy time at apple i mean just  hyper growth and i got responsibilities  very quickly and just really got a taste  for on the entrepreneurial and  innovative and product innovation all  those things so i wanted to stick with  that when I left Apple I took a year off  and and decided that I really wanted to  try to run something I felt like I had  the skills to be a CEO I wanted to try  to be a CEO and I came back specifically  looking for a CEO opportunity in a very  small start-up and that's when I met my  business partner Jeff who I'm now on my  third company with and as co-founder pom  and then you know back to kind of circle  back to the wealth question you know  joining a start-up and getting a a piece  of it is certainly how you have an  opportunity to build wealth so now I was  hired at palm as the CEO so I got kind  of a percentage as a CEO and then in our  second company I was a co-founder so I  had a significantly higher percentage of  the second company as a co-founder and  then ironically enough we sold the  second company back to the first company  and I ended up with more of Palm the  second time around that I had the first  time around which I always loved but  yeah that's that's how it happened and  then you know the company went public  and did well and so I did well and  that's a story
 

